<b>א.</b> “Strengthen yourself like a lion”—the primary valor is against the yetzer hara, as they asked “who is mighty…” He says “like a lion” because a lion’s nature is not to fear any creature—“their roaring does not dismay…”—so too one should not fear his evil inclination even though it is stronger than he.<br>
<br>
<b>ב.</b> “That he should awaken [the dawn]”—superficially this is mere personal alacrity; the point is that one who does a mitzvah through another’s arousal is unlike one who does it himself. Thus our Tur’s wording “I awaken the dawn and the dawn does not awaken me”—why [would one need] the second clause? We find “one sanctifies himself below and they sanctify him above”; likewise there are two kinds of arousal—first arousal from below, then heavenly arousal that assists. David praised himself for reaching such a level that his arousal from below was so great he needed no arousal from above—hence “I awaken the dawn,” like Israel’s kedushah—“like the hind of dawn”—a level for David alone; we should wish merely for arousal from below so that arousal from above may follow. Therefore Shulchan Arukh mentions only “strengthen yourself” so that at minimum one awakens the dawn even without attaining the second level.
